1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,550
The editor experience.

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,600
Every episode so far, we have been logged in as a superuser.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,229
The client will not be.

4
00:00:10,229 --> 00:00:14,124
So this episode starts by creating a user called Casey,

5
00:00:14,124 --> 00:00:17,524
putting them in Wagtail's default editors group,

6
00:00:17,524 --> 00:00:20,640
and looking at the admin through their eyes.

7
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:22,208
Pages work.

8
00:00:22,208 --> 00:00:27,932
But the team, the testimonials and the studio settings all bounce straight back to

9
00:00:27,932 --> 00:00:31,632
the dashboard, and the menu items are not even there.

10
00:00:31,632 --> 00:00:35,750
We spent two episodes building those things for the client,

11
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:38,543
and the client can not open any of them.

12
00:00:38,543 --> 00:00:42,940
The default editors group can edit pages, images and documents,

13
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:44,546
and nothing we defined.

14
00:00:45,346 --> 00:00:49,178
You could fix it by clicking in the groups screen.

15
00:00:49,178 --> 00:00:51,017
That fixes one database.

16
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:55,844
Put it in a data migration and it fixes every copy of the site.

17
00:00:55,844 --> 00:00:59,370
A colleague's laptop, staging, and production.

18
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:02,128
The obvious version looks like this.

19
00:01:02,128 --> 00:01:05,500
Find the permissions, add them to the group.

20
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:08,775
Run it on a fresh database.

21
00:01:08,775 --> 00:01:09,234
Okay.

22
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:11,984
The editors group got nothing.

23
00:01:11,984 --> 00:01:17,118
And yet, ask whether the permissions exist, and they do.

24
00:01:17,918 --> 00:01:23,697
Django creates permission rows in a signal that runs after every migration has

25
00:01:23,697 --> 00:01:24,363
finished.

26
00:01:24,363 --> 00:01:29,253
On a fresh database they do not exist yet when our migration runs.

27
00:01:29,253 --> 00:01:33,180
So the filter finds nothing, the migration says okay,

28
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:38,069
and a moment later the permissions appear, so nothing looks wrong.

29
00:01:38,069 --> 00:01:39,403
Create them first.

30
00:01:39,403 --> 00:01:40,810
And the nasty part.

31
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:44,589
On my existing database, the broken version worked.

32
00:01:44,589 --> 00:01:47,552
It would only have failed in production.

33
00:01:48,352 --> 00:01:52,350
Fixed, and tested on a fresh database this time.

34
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:57,514
All seven permissions, and Casey can open everything we built.

35
00:01:58,314 --> 00:02:00,876
Now a bug we wrote ourselves.

36
00:02:00,876 --> 00:02:04,321
The contact page has two preview modes.

37
00:02:04,321 --> 00:02:06,352
The form previews fine.

38
00:02:06,352 --> 00:02:09,444
The landing page preview redirects.

39
00:02:10,244 --> 00:02:14,507
In episode ten, we turned render landing page into a redirect,

40
00:02:14,507 --> 00:02:16,364
to fix the refresh problem.

41
00:02:16,364 --> 00:02:19,183
Wagtail's preview calls that same method.

42
00:02:19,183 --> 00:02:22,553
So the preview jumped to the live thank you page,

43
00:02:22,553 --> 00:02:27,985
and an editor rewriting the thank you message would only ever see the published

44
00:02:27,985 --> 00:02:28,535
version.

45
00:02:28,535 --> 00:02:31,423
Render the template directly for previews.

46
00:02:31,423 --> 00:02:36,374
I checked it against an unpublished draft, and the draft text now shows.

47
00:02:37,174 --> 00:02:39,374
Now something for the client.

48
00:02:39,374 --> 00:02:43,243
The newest enquiries, right on the admin dashboard.

49
00:02:43,243 --> 00:02:48,553
A small component, registered with the construct homepage panels hook.

50
00:02:48,553 --> 00:02:53,560
Homepage here means the admin's own front page, not the website's.

51
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:57,386
The important line is get forms for user.

52
00:02:57,386 --> 00:03:02,108
It applies exactly the same permission rule as the forms screen.

53
00:03:02,108 --> 00:03:03,732
I checked three users.

54
00:03:03,732 --> 00:03:06,536
The superuser and Casey see the panel.

55
00:03:06,536 --> 00:03:09,488
A user who can only log in sees nothing.

56
00:03:09,488 --> 00:03:15,096
If you queried every submission instead, the dashboard would show enquiries,

57
00:03:15,096 --> 00:03:18,786
names and email addresses, to anyone with a login.

58
00:03:19,586 --> 00:03:22,131
And here is Casey's dashboard.

59
00:03:22,131 --> 00:03:27,644
Studio and Settings in the menu, and recent enquiries at the top.

60
00:03:28,444 --> 00:03:31,408
Every page type now has a page description,

61
00:03:31,408 --> 00:03:35,681
which Wagtail shows when an editor chooses between page types.

62
00:03:35,681 --> 00:03:40,712
And then I went to add a page under Home, and the chooser never appeared.

63
00:03:40,712 --> 00:03:44,917
The journal and the contact page are both at their max count,

64
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:49,604
so only one type is allowed, and Wagtail skips straight to the form.

65
00:03:49,604 --> 00:03:52,774
That is episode seven's rules doing their job.

66
00:03:53,574 --> 00:03:59,185
And every piece of help text we have written since episode three is right there,

67
00:03:59,185 --> 00:04:00,307
under its field.

68
00:04:00,307 --> 00:04:03,744
Including the alt text guidance from episode six.

69
00:04:04,544 --> 00:04:07,058
Commit, and tag.

70
00:04:07,858 --> 00:04:10,776
Next episode, production settings.

71
00:04:10,776 --> 00:04:14,467
Postgres, environment variables, debug off,

72
00:04:14,467 --> 00:04:18,244
and the checklist Django already ships with.

73
00:04:19,044 --> 00:04:24,837
Next episode, production settings.

74
00:04:24,837 --> 00:04:28,244
Thanks for watching.
